.\" frameit(1) manual page. Plain man(7) macros: renders with groff or mandoc,
.\" no scdoc or pandoc needed.
.TH FRAMEIT 1 2026-10-01 "frameit 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
frameit \- temporary selection rectangle overlay for Wayland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B frameit
.RB [ \-c | \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B frameit bind
.RB [ \-c | \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.br
.B frameit \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B frameit
draws a temporary, translucent selection rectangle on the screen. The
rectangle is a real compositor overlay (a layer-shell surface), so it shows up
in screen shares through PipeWire: Discord, Google Meet, OBS, and so on.
Nothing is captured, saved, or left on screen once the gesture ends.
.PP
The gesture is:
.IP 1. 4
Press the trigger chord, by default Super+Shift+Z. Hyprland launches
.BR frameit .
.IP 2. 4
Keep holding the modifiers, press the left mouse button, and drag. The
rectangle follows the pointer.
.IP 3. 4
Release the button. The rectangle disappears and
.B frameit
exits.
.PP
Drag in any direction: the rectangle is always normalised. It stays on the
monitor where the drag started and clamps to that monitor's edges, even if the
pointer crosses to another monitor. Colours, border width, corner radius and
cursor shape come from the configuration file (see
.BR CONFIGURATION ).
.SS Cancelling
Pressing Escape or clicking the right mouse button exits at any time, whether
or not a drag is in progress.
.PP
.B frameit
does not know which keys launched it. When it gains keyboard focus it reads
the modifier keys (Super, Shift, Ctrl, Alt) physically held at that moment and
treats them as the chord that launched it. If every one of those modifiers is
released before the left button is pressed,
.B frameit
disarms and exits, so a trigger you did not mean leaves nothing behind. Once
the left button is down the modifiers may be released freely.
.PP
With a trigger that holds no modifier at that moment, such as a bare key or a
mouse side button like
.BR mouse:276 ,
there is nothing to disarm on, and
.B frameit
stays armed until you drag, press Escape, or right-click.
.PP
Only one instance runs at a time. A second
.B frameit
started while one is already up exits immediately and successfully.
.SS Compositor support
Built for Hyprland.
.B frameit
works on any Wayland compositor that offers the layer-shell, viewporter and
single-pixel-buffer protocols, which covers wlroots-based compositors such as
sway and river. The cursor-shape protocol is used when present (see
.B cursor
below).
.SH COMMANDS
Without a command,
.B frameit
runs the overlay.
.TP
.B bind
Register the configured
.B trigger
chord with the running Hyprland so that it launches this executable. The chord
is split on "+": the last part is the key, the rest are modifiers.
.B frameit
first tries
.BR "hyprctl keyword bind" ;
when Hyprland answers that the Lua API must be used instead, it falls back to
.B hyprctl eval
with an equivalent
.B hl.bind
call. The bind uses the absolute path of the running executable, so Hyprland
does not need
.B frameit
on its PATH. It is a runtime bind, not part of the config file: if a config
reload drops it, run
.B frameit bind
again, or start it from your Hyprland config (see
.BR HYPRLAND ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-config " PATH
Read the configuration from
.I PATH
instead of the default location. Applies to both the overlay and
.BR bind .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration file is optional. It is read from
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/frameit/config.toml ,
falling back to
.I \(ti/.config/frameit/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset, unless
.B \-\-config
names another file. A missing file means the defaults.
.PP
The format is a flat list of
.B key = value
lines. Comments start with
.B #
outside double quotes. Values may be wrapped in double quotes, which are
stripped. An unknown key or a bad value prints a warning on standard error
naming the file and line, and keeps the default for that key; the rest of the
file is still applied, so a typo never leaves the tool unusable.
.PP
All sizes are logical pixels, so the rectangle looks the same on a scale-2
monitor as on a scale-1 monitor.
.TP
.B fill
Fill colour, as
.B #rrggbb
or
.BR #rrggbbaa .
The leading
.B #
is optional, and a colour without an alpha component is opaque.
Default "#4287f540", a translucent blue.
.TP
.B border
Border colour, same format as
.BR fill .
Default "#4287f5", an opaque blue.
.TP
.B border_width
Border width, an integer from 0 to 64. Default 2.
.TP
.B border_radius
Corner radius, an integer from 0 to 256; 0 gives square corners. Default 0.
.TP
.B cursor
Pointer shape while
.B frameit
is up: one of
.BR crosshair ,
.B default
or
.B hidden
.RB ( none
is accepted as an alias for
.BR hidden ).
.B crosshair
and
.B default
need the compositor's cursor-shape protocol and leave the cursor unchanged
without it;
.B hidden
works everywhere. Default "crosshair".
.TP
.B trigger
Key chord registered by
.BR "frameit bind" ,
written as modifiers and a key joined by "+", for example
"SUPER + SHIFT + Z". The overlay itself does not read it.
Default "SUPER + SHIFT + Z".
.PP
An example with every key:
.PP
.EX
fill = "#4287f540"        # translucent blue
border = "#4287f5"        # opaque blue
border_width = 2          # logical pixels
border_radius = 6         # logical pixels, 0 for square corners
cursor = "crosshair"      # crosshair, default or hidden
trigger = "SUPER + SHIFT + Z"
.EE
.SH HYPRLAND
There are two ways to bind the trigger.
.SS In your Hyprland config
Lua, for example in
.IR \(ti/.config/hypr/keybinds.lua :
.PP
.EX
hl.bind("SUPER + SHIFT + Z", hl.dsp.exec_cmd("frameit"))
.EE
.PP
Classic
.IR hyprland.conf :
.PP
.EX
bind = SUPER SHIFT, Z, exec, frameit
.EE
.PP
Make sure the directory holding
.B frameit
is on the PATH that Hyprland sees, or use the full path in the bind.
.SS In frameit's config
Set
.B trigger
in the configuration file and have Hyprland run
.B frameit bind
at startup, which registers that chord through
.BR hyprctl .
Lua, inside your \fBhl.on("hyprland.start", ...)\fR block:
.PP
.EX
hl.exec_cmd("frameit bind")
.EE
.PP
Classic:
.PP
.EX
exec = frameit bind
.EE
.SS Choosing a chord
Any chord works, with one constraint: the modifiers you are still holding when
you press the left mouse button must not form a mouse bind of their own.
Hyprland evaluates mouse binds before it passes a press to any surface, and
\fBSUPER + mouse:272\fR (Super with the left button) is the window-drag bind
in most configs. That is why Super+Z does not work as a trigger unless window
dragging is moved to another chord. Super+Shift with the left button is
normally free.
.PP
The one trigger that cannot work is the left mouse button itself. When a
focus-grabbing layer surface maps, Hyprland synthesises a release for every
button currently held and then drops the real release when it arrives, so a
tool launched on that press never sees the button go up.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/frameit/config.toml
Configuration file; see
.BR CONFIGURATION .
Falls back to
.I \(ti/.config/frameit/config.toml
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/frameit.lock
Lock that keeps a single instance running. Falls back to the system temporary
directory when
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is unset.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/frameit/examples/config.toml
Annotated example configuration installed by the package
.RI ( examples/config.toml
in the source tree).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The rectangle was shown and dismissed or cancelled, another instance was
already running, the bind was registered, or the usage summary was printed.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error: the Wayland connection failed, the compositor lacks a required
protocol or advertises no outputs, or
.B hyprctl
could not be run or refused the bind. The reason is printed on standard error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown argument, or
.B \-\-config
without a path.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR Hyprland (1),
.BR hyprctl (1)
.PP
Project page:
